  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nIn regard to precipitation chances today, CAMS show some returns \ncoming through later this morning. RRFS/RAP and HRRR BUFKIT profiles \nindicate moisture associated with this is AOA 12kft so little more \nthan a sprinkle. Not much impact associated with this very subtle \nnorthwest flow wave otherwise, with weak high pressure moving east, \nreturn southeast low level winds for today.  Clouds depart mid-day \nto the afternoon, so favorable daytime heating, but the wind \ndirection is less than favorable for mixing.\n\nCAMS still indicate warm advection elevated convection potential, \nbut have shifted it more towards south central/southeast South \nDakota. RRFS still has a moist/warm tongue at about 800mb, with \nconvergence with steep lapse rates aloft. Something to monitor but \nunlikely to impact our area now. \n\nFor Friday, a plume of humid air comes up with the persistent south \nwinds. NBM dewpoints top out around 60F, which will limit the fire \ndanger while mixed winds top out around 20kts. \n\nSaturday should be the warmest day for most of the area. First \nthough, 1/2km winds increase out of the southwest to around 45kts \nwith the core across the east/western Minnesota. This will likewise \nenhance downslope conditions with that warm air surge, though with \nonly 45kts in the critical layer and unidirectional flow aloft \ntranslates to about 45 to 50 mph. In regard to just below record \ntemperatures, GEFS 850mb thermal anomaly is still above 2 standard \ndeviations above climo. Southwest low level flow will likewise \nenhance mixing, and we'll probably exceed the mean NBM which \nis currently running in the mid 80s. \n\nStill looking like the wave for Monday is embedded within the \nnorthern stream and the cut off low over the 4-Corners fails to re-\nintegrate until late in the 180 hour model timescale. Thus, while we \nretain POPs, overall rainfall amounts are fairly limited.  \n\nMuch cooler air follows, especially when in comparison to the \nprevious days. The difference between Saturdays and Mondays NBM \nhighs is around 30 degrees.",
